# Multi-Scale Coincidence Detector
# Detects timing relationships between two input streams.
# Both sensory channels converge on a shared trigger population.

Define a network named coincidence_detector with timestep 0.0016.
# Network with 2 inputs, 3 LIF neurons, 2 linear transforms, 1 output.
# flow: input_a → sensory_A → trigger → output
#        input_b → sensory_B ↗

# Layers:
Define an input port named input_a with shape (10,).
Define an input port named input_b with shape (10,).
Define a LIF neuron named sensory_A with time constant 0.01, resistance 1.0, leak voltage 0.0, and firing threshold 1.0.
Define a LIF neuron named sensory_B with time constant 0.01, resistance 1.0, leak voltage 0.0, and firing threshold 1.0.
Define a linear transformation named w_sensory_A_trigger with weight matrix shape (10, 10) annotated with metadata weight_init equal to "xavier" annotated with metadata seed equal to 42.
Define a linear transformation named w_sensory_B_trigger with weight matrix shape (10, 10) annotated with metadata weight_init equal to "xavier" annotated with metadata seed equal to 43.
Define a LIF neuron named trigger with time constant 0.02, resistance 1.0, leak voltage 0.0, and firing threshold 0.8.
Define an output port named output with shape (10,).

# Connections:
input_a connects to sensory_A.
input_b connects to sensory_B.
sensory_A connects to w_sensory_A_trigger.
w_sensory_A_trigger connects to trigger.
sensory_B connects to w_sensory_B_trigger.
w_sensory_B_trigger connects to trigger.
trigger connects to output.

# Note: AMPA/NMDA receptor dynamics are dropped — not representable in NIR.
# Future extension: encode receptor time constants as metadata on weight nodes.
